            <ArticleTitle>Phenoxyphenyl pyridines as novel state-dependent, high-potency sodium channel inhibitors.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>4277-85</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the search for more efficacious drugs to treat neuropathic pain states, a series of phenoxyphenyl pyridines was designed based on 4-(4-flurophenoxy)benzaldehyde semicarbazone. Through variation of the substituents on the pyridine ring, several potent state-dependent sodium channel inhibitors were identified. From these compounds, 23 dose dependently reversed tactile allodynia in the Chung model of neuropathic pain. Administered orally at 10 mg/kg the level of reversal was ca. 50%, comparable to the effect of carbamazepine administered orally at 100 mg/kg.</AbstractText>
